{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His early rule was marked by instability due to his minority and the power struggles within the County of Tripoli.\n\n**highlighted section part 1**\n\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by the forces of Nur ad-Din at the Battle of Harim. He was held captive for nearly ten years until a large ransom was paid in 1173. During his captivity Tripoli was ruled by his mother Hodierna and later by his cousin Bohemond III of Antioch. Upon his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and resumed his rule. He played a significant role in the politics of the Crusader states during the reign of Baldwin IV of Jerusalem.\n\n**highlighted section part 2**\n\nRaymond III was a key figure in the faction opposing the influence of the Knights Templar and the royal court of Jerusalem. He was involved in the conspiracy to crown Baldwin IVs half-brother Baldwin of Montferrat in 1183. This move was intended to reduce the power of Guy of Lusignan who had married Sibylla of Jerusalem. Raymonds actions reflected his desire to maintain a balance of power among the Crusader states.\n\n**highlighted section part 3**\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 without a direct heir. His death marked the end of the direct line of Raymond II of Tripolis rule over the county. His death also coincided with the disastrous Battle of Hattin which led to the loss of Jerusalem to Saladin. Raymonds legacy includes his efforts to navigate the complex political landscape of the Crusader states while maintaining the autonomy of Tripoli. His actions both during his captivity and after his release had lasting impacts on the region.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*